  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Binga Bank (Binga State Bank)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago's first Black-owned bank: founded as a private bank by Jesse Binga in 1908 (drawing $200,000+ in deposits on opening day) and chartered as the Binga State Bank in 1921 \u2014 the first Black-owned state-chartered bank in Chicago. Headquartered at 35th & South State Streets with the five-story Arcade Building (1929), it served a community shut out of white-owned banks. The bank closed in 1930 in the Great Depression; Binga was convicted of embezzlement in 1933, served three years, and died in Chicago in 1950. Neighborhood: Bronzeville."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Binga State Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Jesse Binga's pioneering Black-owned Chicago bank. Neighborhood: Bronzeville. Years active: 1908\u20131930."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Community Bank of Lawndale / Covenant Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "First Black-owned bank on Chicago's West Side (1977), founded by Rev. Shelvin Jerome Hall; bought in 2008 by Living Word Christian Center with investments from 3,000+ church members and renamed Covenant Bank; closed by regulators 2013. Neighborhood: North Lawndale."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Covenant Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Faith-capitalized Black community bank on Chicago's West Side (2008-2013); bought from Community Bank of Lawndale by Rev. Bill Winston, funded by thousands of small church-linked shareholders, closed by regulators with deposits assumed by Liberty Bank and Trust. Neighborhood: North Lawndale. Years active: 1977\u20132013."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Douglass National Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Douglass National Bank was a Black-owned bank founded by Anthony Overton and named for Frederick Douglass, operating from the Overton Hygienic Building at 3619\u20133627 S. State Street in Bronzeville. A Library of Congress research guide, citing Robert E. Weems Jr.'s The Merchant Prince of Black Chicago (2020), identifies it as the second nationally chartered Black-owned bank in the United States; CTL's banking brief flags that an NPS-derived source in the same corpus calls it the first, and this record carries the contradiction openly rather than resolving it by preference. The Kansas City Fed da"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Highland Community Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Black-owned community bank on Chicago's South Side (1970-2015); closed January 23, 2015 by Illinois regulators for operating in an unsafe and unsound condition with inadequate capital; all deposits assumed by United Fidelity Bank of Evansville, Indiana. Neighborhood: South Side Chicago. Years active: 1970-2015."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Illinois Service Federal Savings & Loan Association?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Black-owned Chicago thrift founded 1934 when 13 Black South Side leaders pooled $7,000 to make mortgages where white-owned banks would not lend; founders included property manager (later CHA chairman) Robert Taylor and insurance executive A.W. Williams (35 years, president and board chairman). Main office at 4619 S. Martin Luther King Drive, Bronzeville. Demutualized and recapitalized by Ghana's Groupe Nduom in 2016, renamed GN Bank in 2018. NOTE: this record duplicates CTL-E-1251 ('Illinois Service Federal Savings and Loan / ISF Bank / GN Bank'); both are retained per the append-only rule pen"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Illinois Service Federal Savings and Loan / ISF Bank / GN Bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Illinois Service Federal Savings and Loan \u2014 founded 1934 by 13 Black men during the Great Migration \u2014 was Chicago's historic Black-owned thrift at 4619 S. Martin Luther King Drive in Bronzeville. Acquired by Groupe Nduom in April 2016 after a voluntary supervisory conversion, it was rebranded GN Bank in July 2018, remaining African-American-owned. Neighborhood: Bronzeville."
   }
  },
  {
