   "name": "What is First Baptist Congregational Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Historic Black congregation; its building at 60 N Ashland Av is a designated Chicago Landmark (built 1869-71). Neighborhood: Near West Side. Years active: 1869-71\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is First Church of Deliverance?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Metropolitan Spiritual Churches of Christ congregation at 4315 S. Wabash Ave., Chicago (Bronzeville). Founded 1929 by Rev. Clarence H. Cobbs; pioneer of Black religious radio (1935) and television (1953); famed 200-member choir and gospel recordings; at its peak among the largest Black congregations in Chicago. Neighborhood: Bronzeville. Years active: 1929."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is First Presbyterian Church of Chicago?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "First Presbyterian Church of Chicago at 6400 S. Kimbark Ave., Woodlawn. Founded June 26, 1833 \u2014 the oldest continuously operating institution in Chicago. Current Gothic Revival building (Thomas Tallmadge & Vernon S. Watson) built 1927; the first public school and first Head Start programs in the region started in its buildings; hosted the Shrine of Christ the King congregation after the October 2015 fire. Neighborhood: Woodlawn."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Greater Harvest Missionary Baptist Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "South Side church associated with the Roberta Martin gospel tradition."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Greater Mount Hope Baptist Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Baptist congregation at 6035 Princeton Street, Englewood (presently Mount Hope Baptist Church). Under pastor Rev. William Lambert \u2014 a 1962 Chicago delegate to Albany, Georgia, and a Clergy Alliance of Chicago leader in the selective-buying campaign \u2014 the church supported the development of Operation Breadbasket and in August 1966 became the South Side Action Center of the Chicago Freedom Movement. Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: 1966-1967."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Greater Mount Hope Baptist Church / Chicago Freedom Movement South Side Action Center?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
