   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Life Center COGIC?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Life Center COGIC \u2014 the Life Center Church of God in Christ, known to its congregation as the Prayer Palace \u2014 is a Pentecostal congregation at 5500 South Indiana Avenue in Chicago's Washington Park neighborhood, led for nearly five decades by Pastor T. L. Barrett. Barrett began pastoring in 1966 and led Mt. Zion Baptist Church, at 5512 South Indiana Avenue, from the late 1960s; after a theological disagreement with that church's elders, he left in 1976 to form his own ministry, the Life Center COGIC church, in the same neighborhood. The congregation is the institutional home of Barrett's youth"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Living Word Christian Center?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Living Word Christian Center is a nondenominational Word of Faith congregation in Forest Park, Illinois, founded by Dr. Bill Winston and his wife, Dr. Veronica Winston. The ministry began in 1988 with weekend Faith Crusades run from Minneapolis; the Winstons moved to Chicago that December and opened a Bible training school at the Quality Inn at Madison and Halsted, becoming the Living Word Church of Chicago in 1989 and Living Word Christian Center in 1990. After meeting at Forest Park's Chez Rou\u00e9 Banquet Hall from 1994 to 1997, the church bought the declining 33-acre Forest Park Mall and held "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Metropolitan Apostolic Community Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Apostolic congregation at 4100 S. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Drive, Chicago (Grand Boulevard), in a Richardsonian Romanesque building (1890; 1913) designated a Chicago Landmark in 2007. Descends from the interracial, nonsectarian Metropolitan Community Church (1920), which hosted Ida B. Wells-Barnett, Robert S. Abbott, Gwendolyn Brooks, the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters, and Eleanor Roosevelt. Led for 20 years by senior pastor Rev. Dr. Leon D. Finney Jr. (d. 2020), Woodlawn organizer and developer. Neighborhood: Grand Boulevard. Years active: 1920\u2013present."
   }
  },
