    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago Catholic parish on 47th Street where George Clements was associate pastor, 1957-1962."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Benedict the African Parish (Roman Catholic)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Roman Catholic parish at 340 W. 66th St., Englewood, formed from the merger of eight parishes as the neighborhood became predominantly African-American; two worship sites became separate parishes in 1995 and re-merged in 2016. Distinctive African-hut-inspired architecture; included in Chicago Architecture Foundation Open House Chicago. Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: 1968-1995; 1995-2016; 2016-present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Dorothy Parish?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago Catholic parish on 78th Street where George Clements was associate pastor, 1962-1969."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Edmund's Episcopal Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Predominantly Black Episcopal parish at 6105 S. Michigan Avenue, Chicago (Washington Park). Began 1905 as the Washington Park Mission; admitted as a parish with charter filed October 12, 1940. One of Chicago's oldest active Black Episcopalian congregations; its archives (1930-2002) are held in the CPL Vivian G. Harsh Research Collection. Neighborhood: South Side Chicago; Washington Park. Years active: 1905-."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Mary's M.B. Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "St. Mary's Missionary Baptist Church is located at 1040 W. Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: unknown-present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Rita of Cascia Parish?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Roman Catholic (Augustinian) parish at 6243 S. Fairfield Ave., Chicago Lawn, founded 1905 \u2014 described as the first Augustinian church in Chicago. United with St. Clare of Montefalco Parish effective July 1, 2020 under Renew My Church; serves a primarily Latino community. Associated St. Rita of Cascia High School (all-boys, founded 1905) remains in operation. Neighborhood: Chicago Lawn (community area 66)."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is St. Sabina Catholic Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "St. Sabina Catholic Church is a parish of the Archdiocese of Chicago in Auburn Gresham, founded in July 1916 when Archbishop George W. Mundelein sent Rev. Thomas Egan to establish it; Egan celebrated the first Mass on July 9, 1916, in a storefront at 7915 South Ashland Avenue. Built up by Irish Catholic parishioners, it became one of Chicago's largest parishes, then nearly closed during the white-flight era. From 1981 its pastor has been Fr. Michael Pfleger \u2014 a white priest, appointed associate pastor in 1975 and, at thirty-one, the archdiocese's youngest pastor \u2014 under whom the predominantly "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Stone Temple Baptist Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Historic Black congregation; its building at 3620-3624 W. Douglas Blvd is a designated Chicago Landmark (built 1926). Neighborhood: North Lawndale. Years active: 1926\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Trinity United Church of Christ?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Prominent South Side congregation led for decades by Rev. Jeremiah Wright. Neighborhood: Chicago. Years active: 1960s\u2013contemporary."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Truevine Missionary Baptist Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Historic Black congregation; its building at 6720 S Stewart Av is a designated Chicago Landmark (built 1892). Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: 1892\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Victory Apostolic Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Victory Apostolic Church is an Apostolic Pentecostal congregation founded by the Rev. Andrew D. Singleton Jr. Its first service was held on August 4, 1996, in Harvey, Illinois, where the young church met at a former Ramada Inn; a year later it moved to Chicago Heights, where it worshipped for twelve years and grew rapidly. In February 2009 Victory moved to its present home in Matteson, Illinois \u2014 a 12-acre campus with a 2,500-seat sanctuary, built at a reported cost of $20 million, the figures the church itself publishes. Singleton, who was mentored by Bishop Arthur M. Brazier of Chicago's Apo"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Warren Avenue Congregational Church?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Warren Avenue Congregational Church at 3101 W. Warren Boulevard, East Garfield Park (the building now houses New Greater St. John Community Missionary Baptist Church). In 1966 it welcomed the Rev. Martin Luther King Jr. and SCLC, becoming headquarters of the Chicago Freedom Movement's West Side campaign against slum housing."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Alpha Suffrage Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Alpha Suffrage Club was a Chicago organization founded on January 30, 1913, by journalist and anti-lynching crusader Ida B. Wells, with white colleagues Belle Squire and Virginia Brooks. Illinois Institute of Technology's institutional history describes it as the first organization established to promote the right for Black women to vote in Illinois, formed because Black women had been largely excluded from existing local and national suffrage organizations. The club met every Wednesday evening at the Negro Fellowship League, at 3005 South State Street in Bronzeville, and set out to educat"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Andy's Jazz Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago jazz club. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Appomattox Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Elite Black social and civic club of Chicago, organized circa 1900 in connection with Edward Wright as \u201cblack Chicago's version of the Commercial Club.\u201d Named for the Virginia courthouse where the Civil War effectively ended, it was a political and social powerhouse where Black business and political leaders gathered to talk policy and influence elections. From 1920 it occupied a three-story stone mansion at 3632 Grand Boulevard / South Parkway (now Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Drive; building no longer stands); the address served as headquarters for the 1940 American Negro Exposition planning. "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is B.L.U.E.S.?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago blues club at 2519 N. Halsted St., opened 1979 and known by its B.L.U.E.S. marquee. It books primarily Chicago musicians \u2014 elder statesmen such as Eddie Shaw and Jimmy Johnson alongside younger club-circuit artists (Jimmy Burns, Mike Wheeler, Billy Branch, Little Ed) \u2014 in a friendly rivalry with Kingston Mines across the street. \u201cB.L.U.E.S. keeps its offering to blues and booze.\u201d Owner Robert Hecko, partner Bill Gilmore, manager Jennifer Littleton (per Chicago Blues Hall of Fame listings). Neighborhood: Chicago; Lincoln Park. Years active: 1979\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Birdland (formerly the Beige Room)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Jazz room in the Pershing Hotel complex at 6400 S. Cottage Grove, Woodlawn \u2014 the former Beige Room. The Pershing Hotel's Beige Room opened where El Grotto Supper Club had been (1944\u20131947 at 6412 S. Cottage Grove), per the Chicago Defender, Dec. 13, 1947. Arnett Cobb played the room (Defender, Dec. 24, 1955). The complex also held the Pershing Ballroom, the Pershing Lounge (Ahmad Jamal, 1957), and the basement club Budland \u2014 first a jazz room featuring Arnett Cobb, Miles Davis, and Billie Holiday \u2014 anchoring the 63rd\u2013Cottage Grove nightlife strip. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1940s\u2013195"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Bud Billiken Club (Chicago Defender youth section)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Defender's youth club/section built around the Bud Billiken character; between 1930 and 1934 approximately 10,000 children's names appeared in the section and were archived at the Carter G. Woodson Regional Library."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Checkerboard Lounge?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "South Side blues club long associated with Buddy Guy. Neighborhood: Chicago; Hyde Park. Years active: 1970s\u20132000s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Club Bagdad?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "South Side jazz club at 840 E. 63rd Street, documented in the Chicago Defender's October 10, 1953 nightlife listings (the 5 Blazes and Terry Timmons). Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1953. Status recorded October 2026: Unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Club DeLisa?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Famed Bronzeville nightclub whose floor shows starred Black entertainers. Neighborhood: Bronzeville; Chicago. Years active: 1930s\u20131950s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Cotton Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "1950s Woodlawn jazz club at 6249 S. Cottage Grove Avenue, documented in the October 10, 1953 Chicago Defender (Harold King and Red Allen). A namesake of the Harlem Cotton Club; distinct from both the New York original and the later 1986-2006 South Loop club. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1953. Status recorded October 2026: Unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Den One?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago gay club where Ron Hardy began his DJ career in 1974 with two turntables, a mixer, and a reel-to-reel tape deck. Years active: 1974."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is El Grotto Supper Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Supper club at 6412 S. Cottage Grove, in Chicago's Pershing Hotel (Woodlawn), operating 1944-1947. Hosted Ivie Anderson, saxophonist George Auld, and singer Ida James; billed as 'Harry Fields' El Grotto Supper Club'; served dinner to the Billikens; fielded a prize-winning float (1946). The Pershing Hotel's Beige Room opened in its space in December 1947. Documented in the University of Chicago Library's 'Researching Chicago Jazz Venues' guide from Chicago Defender citations. Neighborhood: Woodlawn (64th & Cottage Grove). Years active: 1944\u20131947. Status recorded October 2026: Closed 1947."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Grand Terrace Cafe?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Bronzeville nightclub where Earl Hines's orchestra held long residencies. Neighborhood: Bronzeville; Chicago. Years active: 1930s\u20131940s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Green Door Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Englewood, Chicago club documented in a 1959 community-history photograph (photographer unknown) shown in the Living Tracks 'ENT Community Meeting #2' presentation. Its exact street address and operator are unverified in located sources. Not the Green Door Tavern of River North (678 N Orleans St). Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: 1959. Status recorded October 2026: Unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is High Chaparral?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Clarence Ludd's large South Side soul club at 7740 S. Stony Island Ave., near the northwest corner of 79th and Stony Island; one of the most popular Black clubs of the 1960s and 1970s, hosting local and national icons. Neighborhood: 79th Street commercial corridor (Chatham / Greater Grand Crossing)."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is House of Blues Chicago?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "1,400-capacity concert hall at 329 N. Dearborn Street in the Marina City complex, opened 1996; a Live Nation venue hosting Buddy Guy's annual January residency and the Sunday World Famous Gospel Brunch. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Ida B. Wells Woman's Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Black women's civic club organized by Ida B. Wells in 1893 as the Women's Era Club and later renamed in her honor; first-of-its-kind for African-American women in Chicago. Charter member of the Cook County Women's Clubs; campaigned for the Ida B. Wells housing project (1939). Years active: 1890s, 1900s, 1910s, 1920s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is International Game Room?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Calvin Hollis' International Game Room on 47th Street off King Drive, Chicago; the venue where promoter David Risqu\u00e9 gave DJ Celeste Alexander her first DJ gig in the early 1980s. Neighborhood: Bronzeville. Years active: 1980s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Jazz Showcase?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago's oldest jazz club (founded 1947 by Joe Segal); 170-seat listening room at 806 S Plymouth Ct (Joe Segal Way), South Loop, in rebuilt Dearborn Station since 2008. Live seven nights a week. Not Black-owned (Segal family); stage, archive, and audience history inseparable from Black artistry. Neighborhood: Chicago. Years active: 1947\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Kingston Mines?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Lincoln Park blues club at 2548 N. Halsted Street, opened 1968; the oldest continuously operating blues club in Chicago. Grew out of the Kingston Mines Theatre Company (the original Grease premiered there in 1971); two stages; owned by the Pellegrino family. Neighborhood: Chicago. Years active: 1968\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Kitty Kat Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Gay nightclub at 611 E. 63rd Street in Woodlawn, a haven for gay South Siders with forward-looking jazz programming (Ahmad Jamal, John Young, King Fleming before their ascents). Two scenes of A Raisin in the Sun (1961) were filmed there. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1953\u20131961+. Status recorded October 2026: Closed; fate unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Lee's Unleaded Blues?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "South Side blues club at 7401 S. South Chicago Avenue in Greater Grand Crossing, opened 1983 by Ray and Leola 'Lee' Grey on the site of the 1970s Queen Bee's Lounge; closed 2015, reopened 2024 under Warren Berger. Neighborhood: Greater Grand Crossing. Years active: 1983\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Off The Street Club?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Off The Street Club is a youth organization in West Garfield Park, Chicago, founded in 1900 \u2014 by the organization's own account \u2014 and operating continuously into the present. It works as a youth safe-space and development club for West Side youth, offering athletics, tutoring, mentoring, arts programming, and camp. CultureTechLens's institutional research keeps the club for its longevity finding while flagging two cautions: it was founded in 1900, before its neighborhood was Black, and its current governance and funding are unverified in the research record. Years active: 1900s, 1910s, 1920s, "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Pershing Hotel club (Pershing Lounge / Budland)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Basement jazz club Budland (later R&B) in the Pershing Hotel at 6400 S. Cottage Grove, Woodlawn. Site of Ahmad Jamal's landmark 1958 live recordings. Hosted Arnett Cobb, Miles Davis, and Billie Holiday in its jazz years. Neighborhood: Woodlawn (64th & Cottage Grove). Years active: 1957\u20131958+; 1957+. Status recorded October 2026: Closed; fate unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Phyllis Wheatley Club and Home?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Black women's club and home for young Black women (founded 1896, led by Elizabeth Lindsay Davis); final home at 5128 S. Michigan Ave., Bronzeville; lost to court-ordered demolition after the 2021 endangered listing. Neighborhood: Bronzeville. Years active: 1896-1970s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Playboy Club (Chicago)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago Playboy Club; Hugh Hefner booked Dick Gregory there in 1961, launching his national career."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Roberts Show Lounge?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Herman Roberts's South Side show lounge; starred Nina Simone, Sam Cooke, and Dinah Washington. Years active: 1950s\u20131970s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Rosa's Lounge?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Family-run blues club at 3420 W Armitage Ave, opened 1984 by Italian immigrant Tony Mangiullo and named for his mother Rosa. Opened as a tribute to the South Side blues lounges such as Theresa's Lounge; a community anchor for Chicago blues, still programming live blues as of 2026. Neighborhood: Chicago. Years active: 1980s\u2013contemporary."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Shelter (house club)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago underground house/loft-scene nightclub that opened in 1990 and legitimized the city's post-Warehouse party circuit; distinct from New York's Club Shelter. Years active: 1990\u20131990s."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Smart Bar?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Smartbar opened in July 1982 at 3730 N. Clark under Metro owner Joe Shanahan, with Frankie Knuckles reportedly the first DJ. Neighborhood: Chicago. Years active: 1982\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Stage Lounge?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chuck Berry, Art Blakey bookings in the 1950s. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1955\u2013?. Status recorded October 2026: Unknown."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is The Economic Club of Chicago?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chicago civic organization. Mellody Hobson became the first African-American woman to head it in 2017."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is The Rhumboogie?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Bronzeville nightclub owned by Charlie Glenn; hosted Charlie Parker and T-Bone Walker. Neighborhood: Bronzeville."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is The Warehouse?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Warehouse was a DJ-led dance club at 206 S. Jefferson Street in Chicago's West Loop, in a three-story loft building, and is the nightclub widely credited as the birthplace of house music. Promoter Robert Williams opened the club in 1977 and installed New York DJ Frankie Knuckles as resident DJ in March 1977; Knuckles's residency ran to 1982. The room drew a Black, Latino, and largely gay crowd, and CTL's venue research grades it as one of the first clubs where gay and straight, Black, Latinx, and white clubgoers shared a dance floor. The best-supported etymology of \"house music\" derives th"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is 63rd Street Theatre (Langley Hippodrome)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Early 63rd Street vaudeville house. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1911\u20131949. Status recorded October 2026: Demolished."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Afro-Arts Theater?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Regional theater at 3900 S. Drexel on Chicago's South Side, opened 1967 by musician/activist Kelan Philip Cohran. A meeting place for Black power activists and, per the Journal of Black Studies, central to the growth of African American consciousness in Chicago, it hosted a weekly cultural extravaganza featuring poets Haki Madhubuti, Carolyn Rodgers, and Useni Eugene Perkins. Gwendolyn Brooks received there on December 28, 1969 what she considered \u201cthe most stirring and significant tribute of her life.\u201d Theater as political infrastructure. Neighborhood: Grand Boulevard. Years active: 1967\u2013."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Auditorium Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Roosevelt University; Adler & Sullivan. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Avalon Regal Theater?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "John Eberson's 1927 Moorish Revival atmospheric theater at 1645 E. 79th St. in South Shore, opened as the Avalon; renamed the New Regal in 1987 by Edward and Bettiann Gardner in honor of the demolished Bronzeville Regal; designated a Chicago Landmark in 1992; purchased by Jerald Gary in 2014 and recommended for a $249,500 Adopt-A-Landmark grant in October 2026. Neighborhood: South Shore. Years active: 1927\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Black Cinema House?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Rebuild Foundation site in Chicago hosting film screenings and discussions. Years active: 2010s-present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Black Ensemble Theater?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Black theater company at 4450 N. Clark St., Uptown, founded 1976 by Jackie Taylor; mission: eradicate racism through theater arts and community engagement. Neighborhood: Chicago; Woodlawn. Years active: 1970s\u2013contemporary."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Chicago Latino Theater Alliance (CLATA)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Alliance founded in 2016 by executive director Myrna Salazar with Chicago's three most prominent Latino arts organizations: the National Museum of Mexican Art, the International Latino Cultural Center, and the Puerto Rican Arts Alliance. A nonprofit 501(c)(3), it produces Destinos, the Chicago International Latino Theater Festival, aiming to build the country's leading international Latino theater festival while giving local companies organizational, marketing, and financial support and working toward a permanent home for Chicago's Latino theater artists. The 3rd Destinos (2019) presented 93+ "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Chicago Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "State Street movie palace; concerts. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Cinema/Chicago?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Cinema/Chicago is the Chicago nonprofit cultural and educational organization that presents the Chicago International Film Festival. Founded in 1964 \u2014 its founder, Michael Kutza, served as its chief executive until his retirement at the end of 2018 and remains CEO Emeritus \u2014 the organization states its mission as fostering better communication between people of diverse cultures through the art of film and the moving image, and serving Chicago's diverse and under-served audiences with access to world-class cinema. Beyond the October festival, Cinema/Chicago runs year-round programs: the CineYou"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Collaboraction Theatre Company?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Collaboraction Theatre Company (also recorded as Collaboraction) is a Chicago theater company devoted to social-issue-driven contemporary theater. Its documented mainstage work in the evidence corpus is the 2019\u201320 season, staged at the Kennedy-King College theaters in Englewood \u2014 the U Building's 292-seat mainstage and black box theaters at 740 W. 63rd Street. The Kennedy-King College theaters' graph record names that season 'We Are Each Other's Business,' including the fourth annual Peacebook Festival and the 'Red Island' world premiere, and places it as year three of Collaboraction's five-y"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Congo Square Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "African American theater company founded in 1999 by Derrick Sanders and Reginald Nelson; one of only two Black Actors' Equity companies in Chicago. Mentored by August Wilson; produced Seven Guitars (2005 Joseph Jefferson Awards sweep) and cultivated Chadwick Boseman (Deep Azure) and Lydia Diamond (Stick Fly). Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Congress Theater?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Logan Square theater; concerts. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Court Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Hyde Park; University of Chicago. Neighborhood: Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Englewood Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Englewood, Chicago movie house: ended burlesque and went movies-only on February 12, 1922 (starting with \u2018Hail the Woman\u2019); staged a public wedding on June 28, 1928; modernized in 1949; still advertising films in 1959. Demolition date unresolved in the sources. Neighborhood: Englewood. Years active: 1922\u20131959. Status recorded October 2026: demolished."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Ethiopian Art Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Short-lived (1922/1923\u20131925) Chicago-based Black theatre company of the Harlem Renaissance, founded by white director Raymond O'Neil and sponsored by Mrs. Sherwood Anderson; its all-Black performers staged European repertoire (Wilde's Salome, a jazz-accompanied Comedy of Errors) and Black-authored work, including Willis Richardson's The Chip Woman's Fortune \u2014 the first Black drama produced on Broadway (Frazee Theatre, May 7, 1923). Years active: 1922/1923\u20131925."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Harris Theater?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Millennium Park venue; taping site of Soul Train Awards 2026. Neighborhood: Chicago."
