   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Small early Woodlawn movie house at 6240 S. Kimbark Avenue (between 62nd and 63rd Streets): opened 1913, seated just under 700, closed in the 1960s, later used as a church, vacant for years, demolished January/February 2016. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1913\u20131960s. Status recorded October 2026: Demolished Jan/Feb 2016."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Marquette Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "1,200-seat movie house at 3157 W. 63rd Street (Kedzie & 63rd) in Chicago Lawn. Designed by Henry L. Newhouse & F.M. Bernham; opened March 23, 1924; leased for 10 years to Balaban & Katz; operated by the Stern Theater Interests circuit; closed January 4, 1981 with Disney's \"The Aristocats\"; demolished August 1987; the site became a Walgreens parking lot. Neighborhood: Chicago Lawn (63rd & Kedzie). Years active: 1924\u20131981. Status recorded October 2026: Demolished Aug 1987; parking lot."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Maryland Theatre?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Maryland Theatre: former movie palace at 855 E. 63rd Street, Chicago (Woodlawn), between S. Drexel and S. Maryland Avenues. Opened in 1928 on the site of the Woodlawn Theatre, seating 1,800. Operated by Balaban & Katz from opening until the 1960s, then part of the ABC Theatres chain. In the 1970s it hosted African-American performing artists. Closed in 1977; vandalized in the 1980s and demolished in the 1990s; the site is now a parking lot. Stood within blocks of the Tivoli Theatre and the Trianon Ballroom. Neighborhood: Woodlawn. Years active: 1928 (or 1918 \u2014 DISPUTED) \u20131977. Status recorded "
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is MPAACT (Ma'at Production Association of Afrikan Centered Theatre)?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Afrikan-centered theater company founded 1990 in Chicago (founding member Shepsu Aakhu): develops new, collaborative Afrikan-centered works through a collective model. Resident playwright Shepsu Aakhu; staged productions at the Greenhouse Theater Center, Lincoln Park (Spoken Word 2020, Swamp Baby 2019, Black and Blue 2018). Neighborhood: Lincoln Park. Years active: 1990\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
