   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Savoy Ballroom?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Savoy Ballroom was a Bronzeville dance hall at 4733 S. Parkway (now Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Drive), at 47th Street, celebrated in the graph record as the \"World's Finest Ballroom\" for Black dancers. It opened in November 1927 as part of a single commercial complex with the Regal Theater and the South Center Department Store, developed by a syndicate headed by Harry M. and Louis Englestein and operated by dance promoter I. Jay Faggen; the complex helped make 47th Street's reputation as the \"Harlem of Chicago.\" The ballroom accommodated more than 4,000 people and anchored the 47th and Sou"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Schorling's Park?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Schorling's Park was a Chicago ballpark on 39th Street between Princeton and Wentworth Streets, at 39th and Wentworth, whose life the Society for American Baseball Research divides into a white major-league act, a Black baseball act, and a final decline. Opened as South Side Park, it hosted its first game on April 21, 1900, as the original Chicago home of Charles Comiskey's White Stockings, later the White Sox. After Comiskey moved his club north, tavern owner John Schorling leased the grounds, rebuilt the stands Comiskey had removed, and in 1911 entered partnership with Rube Foster. The Chica"
   }
  },
