   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Forum at 318 E 43rd Street in Bronzeville, Chicago: an 1897 building that was once an entertainment venue for Chicago's Black artists and musicians; under redevelopment since 2021 (led by Bernard Loyd of Urban Juncture) to return it to a performance venue with banquet hall, retail, and restaurant space. Neighborhood: Bronzeville. Years active: 1897\u2013present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is The Power Plant?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Power Plant was a house-music club at 1015 N. Halsted Street, on Goose Island, founded by Frankie Knuckles in 1983 after he left the Warehouse. The City of Chicago's Warehouse landmark report \u2014 one of this record's own sources \u2014 verifies the address and the founding: when the Warehouse doubled admission in late 1982 and grew more commercial, Knuckles left and established his own club, and his followers came with him. Knuckles DJed at the Power Plant until 1986, when he returned to New York City and established Def Mix with David Morales. Where the Warehouse had been a largely gay, Black an"
   }
  },
  {
